/*Kinda obvious that we should make a linear graph. Try to think of cases of 1-8 where at most 3 edges per layer
(between x and x+1) amortized. ==> Say number of paths from x to n is y, we can easily make y*2 paths from x-1 to n:
consider edges between 2 and 3 have weight 0 and 1, add 0 and 2 weighted edges between 1 to 2, in order to make double
paths. Just doubling is definitely not enough to get any number, but as we have one more edge left per layer, we can
easily add 1 more edge after doubling: this is exactly like binary number system.*/

    int L, n = 20;
    cin >> L;
    vector<Edge> edges;
    int x = __builtin_clz(L)^31;
    edges.emplace_back(n-1, n, 0);
    int cur = 1, u = n-2, v = n-1;
    for (int i = x-1; i >= 0; i--, u--, v--) {
        edges.emplace_back(u, v, 0);
        edges.emplace_back(u, v, cur);
        cur <<= 1;
        if (test(L, i)) edges.emplace_back(u, n, cur), cur++;
    }
    while (v!=1) {
        edges.emplace_back(u, v, 0);
        u--, v--;
    }
    cout << n << ' ' << edges.size() << '\n';
    for (auto &i : edges) {
        cout << i.u << ' ' << i.v << ' ' << i.w << '\n';
    }
}

